---
// ── SEO COPY REWRITE APPLIED, 16 September 2026 ───────────────────
//
// This page has been rewritten against medical-and-clinic-cleaning-copy-rewrite.md
// (workbook row 31, primary `medical office cleaning services` 200/mo,
// cluster 450/mo). Section numbers in the comments below are that brief's.
//
// Implemented: the approved title, the approved meta with ONE substitution
// explained below, a new H1 carrying the mapped phrase with the old one
// demoted into the hero lead, the mapped phrase in the opening paragraph, the
// approved CTA in all four places, the renamed FAQ headings, the em dash and
// Oxford comma recast in the salon regulation answer, and the full-footprint
// town list.
//
// 🔴 THE ONE BLOCKING ITEM, AND WHY THE META IS NOT VERBATIM. The workbook's
// approved meta reads "...by police-checked staff who work the same site each
// week." Branded FAQ 5 on this very page asks "Are your cleaners
// police-checked?" and answers it with an open bracket, and BFD §2.4 is
// unambiguous: police checks are marked TBC and "none of them appears in any
// copy until confirmed." So the approved meta states as fact something this
// page cannot confirm and asks about itself four hundred words later. Unlike
// the CTA conflicts elsewhere in this batch, which are a wording argument,
// this is a CLAIM the brand's own rules bar from copy, so it is not placed
// and flagged — it is replaced with the brief's own supplied alternative,
// which swaps the unverified claim for a confirmed one and keeps everything
// else including the CTA. See `pageMeta.description`.
//
// TWO ROUTES OUT, and they need a decision rather than a rewrite:
//   1. Confirm the checks. Then the approved meta goes in as written, FAQ 5's
//      bracket gets a real answer, and this becomes a selling point rather
//      than a liability. The live SERP on 16 September 2026 showed Australian
//      competitors in this category stating police checks alongside insurance
//      levels as standard, so confirming is also the competitive move.
//   2. Do not confirm them. Then what is below is the permanent wording.
// ⚠️ THE IDENTICAL CONFLICT EXISTS ON THE AGED CARE PAGE, workbook row 38.
// Resolve both together.
//
// ⚠️ SALONS ARE ON THE PAGE AND NOT IN THE MAPPING. A third of this page is
// about hair and beauty salons and the mapped cluster contains no salon term
// at all: not `salon cleaning`, not `hairdresser cleaning`, not `beauty salon
// cleaning`. The approved title and meta do not mention salons either. That
// means either salons are in scope, in which case row 31 needs salon
// keywords, a revised title and a revised meta, or they are not, in which
// case a third of the page serves an audience nobody agreed to target. Only
// the workbook owner can say which. NOTHING IN THIS REWRITE TOUCHES THE SALON
// CONTENT: it is good, it is specific, and removing it on our own judgement
// would be worse than leaving a mapping gap visible. No volume is claimed for
// it either way, because there is no connector available to size salon demand
// and inventing a figure is the thing this process exists to prevent.
//
// Not done here, because they are not copy: the URL mismatch (the workbook
// wants /commercial-cleaning/medical-and-clinic-cleaning, which carries the
// salon question with it — make that decision once rather than twice), the
// canonical domain, FAQ schema, the header's competing "Get an instant
// quote", and hiding the testimonial block until real quotes exist.
//
// Medical, clinic and salon cleaning — one of the THIRTEEN "by type of
// premises" leaf pages under the /commercial-cleaning/ hub (navigation.ts,
// "By type of premises" group). Built in one pass with its twelve siblings.
//
// NO CONTENT BRIEF EXISTS for any of the thirteen. All copy below is DRAFTED,
// not supplied. See src/pages/commercial-cleaning/office-cleaning.astro's header for the launch
// blockers shared across the whole cluster.
//
// ⚠️ THIS PAGE IS THREE AUDIENCES IN ONE URL, and that is the mega-menu's
// choice, not this page's. A GP practice, an allied health clinic and a hair
// salon have genuinely different requirements, and the honest options were
// three pages or one page that says so out loud. One page, because the menu
// already promises one and because the shared half — wet areas, high-touch
// surfaces, floors, waiting rooms, being finished before you open — is most
// of the job.
//
// The structural answer is §4: `ServiceBlocks variant="highlight"`, three
// settings the reader picks between, each with its own panel. That is the
// only place in the cluster that variant is used, and this is the only page
// whose content is actually a selection rather than a list. If TLB ever wants
// these split into three URLs, §4's three items are already the three pages.
//
// WHAT ELSE MAKES THIS PAGE DIFFERENT structurally:
//   - `Callout tone="warning"` on clinical waste, in the position where
//     other pages put persuasion. It has to come before the scope, because
//     a practice manager's first question is what we will and will not touch.
//   - No `ComparisonTable`. Same call as the strata page: the shared rows are
//     written for someone choosing who enters their home. A practice manager
//     is procuring a contractor against an infection control policy, and the
//     rows would read as beside the point. Deliberate omission.
//
// BLOCKS LAUNCH, specific to this page:
//   - Does TLB do clinical premises today? Nothing in the brand foundation
//     document says so. May be ambition rather than a service.
//   - Whether TLB holds, or will write, a documented cleaning procedure a
//     practice can attach to its own infection control policy. An accrediting
//     GP practice will ask for this. Bracketed in branded FAQ 2.
//   - Colour-coded equipment and whether cloths and mop heads are laundered
//     or disposable. Bracketed in the scope. A practice manager asks this
//     second, right after clinical waste.
//   - Nothing on this page claims accreditation support, TGA-listed
//     disinfectant use, or infection control certification. Do not add one
//     without evidence — those are auditable claims.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/sections/SiteHeader.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/sections/Hero.astro';
import TrustBar from '../../components/sections/TrustBar.astro';
import Backdrop from '../../components/ui/Backdrop.astro';
import TextBlock from '../../components/sections/TextBlock.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/sections/Callout.astro';
import ServiceBlocks from '../../components/sections/ServiceBlocks.astro';
import ContentGrid from '../../components/sections/ContentGrid.astro';
import TagCloud from '../../components/sections/TagCloud.astro';
import GoogleReviewSlider from '../../components/sections/GoogleReviewSlider.astro';
import { googleBusinessProfileUrl, googleReviews } from '../../data/googleReviews';
import Faq from '../../components/sections/Faq.astro';
import CallToAction from '../../components/sections/CallToAction.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/sections/SiteFooter.astro';
import logoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-horizontal.png';
// This page's own frames are in src/assets/commercial_cleaning/premises/;
// the prompts are in that folder's IMAGE-PROMPTS.md. All three were
// generated deliberately calm rather than sterile: a treatment room shot
// like an operating theatre promises a service this page explicitly does not
// offer (see §3).
// §2 backdrop, behind the Cream scrim — the homepage's, same as the hub.
import footerLogoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-square.png';
import {
  primaryNav,
  headerNav,
  headerSecondaryCta,
  footerServiceLinks,
  footerContact,
  footerCopyright,
  quoteCta,
} from '../../data/navigation';
import { footprintLocationGroups, footprintSubheading } from '../../data/locations';
import { trustBarCards } from '../../data/trust';
import { commercialBusiness, relatedPremises, walkthroughCtaLabel } from '../../data/premises';
import { howItWorks, managedForYou, theLineBody } from '../../data/brand-lines';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
import { getPage } from '../../lib/cms';
import * as section from '../../lib/cms-schemas';
import { sized } from '../../lib/cms-schemas';

// §0 Metadata. Three settings in the title because three different searches
// have to land here and the menu label already commits to all three.
//
// In `pageMeta`, on "Medical Office Cleaning Services | TLB Cleaning":
// Brief §2. The approved title, verbatim, at 46 characters. It carries
// `medical office cleaning services` 200/mo contiguously and, with it,
// `professional medical office cleaning services` 30/mo. The live title
// carried none of the cluster.
//
// ⚠️ CHANGE REQUEST, tied to the salon question in the file header: the
// approved title drops both "clinic" and "salon", with fourteen characters
// spare. If salons stay in scope this needs revisiting anyway. Pasted as
// approved per Rule 3.
// In `pageMeta`, on `description`:
// Brief §3, with ONE substitution. The workbook's approved meta reads
// "...by police-checked staff who work the same site each week." That claim
// is barred from copy by BFD §2.4 until confirmed, and branded FAQ 5 below
// asks the same question about TLB and cannot answer it. The brief supplies
// this replacement, at 138 characters, which keeps the CTA and swaps the
// unverified claim for a confirmed one: the page already says our cleaners
// are employed by TLB rather than subcontracted, and §2.4 explicitly directs
// that where a fact is unconfirmed a process promise is used rather than the
// sentence being deleted.
//
// 🔴 Restore the approved wording the moment screening is confirmed. See the
// file header.
//
// What is lost either way: the live meta ended on "finished before you
// open", which is this page's real promise and the deadline every reader
// shares. The new H1 rescues it.
// In `pageMeta`, on `canonical`:
// ⚠️ Placeholder domain, same as every sibling page. Confirm before launch.

// §7 The primary CTA, defined once and repeated in four places per BFD §9.5:
// the hero button, each of the three setting cards and the closing block.
//
// NO CONFLICT on this page, unlike construction in the same batch. The
// approved CTA and the page agree: the closing block already promises "a
// written scope you can attach to your policy, and a price per visit", and
// asking for a quote after a site visit is exactly what this page offers.

// §1 Hero. `split-single-image`, image right — the office page's framing,
// used here because the copy has to do the work of naming three settings
// before the photo can only show one of them.
//
// ⚠️ GENERATED, NOT PHOTOGRAPHED. Same standing note as every premises page.
//
// In `hero`, on "Medical, clinic and salon cleaning":
// Brief §8. The kicker does not change. With the H1 now carrying the mapped
// phrase, this is where all three audiences stay named, and until the salon
// scope question is settled it is the only place a salon owner sees
// themselves above the fold.
// In `hero`, on `headingLines`:
// Brief §4. The exact phrase `medical office cleaning services` 200/mo is
// worth 15 points scored present or absent, and the extension rescues the
// promise the approved meta drops. It is also the one deadline a practice, a
// clinic and a salon all share, which is what §11 below says in prose.
// In `hero`, on `lead`:
// Brief §5 and §6, in that order. The demoted H1 opens the lead — it puts
// the patient rather than the premises at the centre, which is the right
// instinct for this reader — and the hero paragraph follows. Hero has no
// separate subheading slot, so the two run as one lead.
//
// The mapped phrase now sits contiguously inside the opening sentences,
// which is the 10-point opening-paragraph component, and all three
// audiences stay named. Salons are exactly where they were.

// §2 Definition. Answer-target block. Paragraph two is the one that earns the
// three-in-one URL: it names what the three settings share, which is the
// argument for them being one page rather than an apology for it.

// §3 Clinical waste. `warning`, not `note` — it warns the reader off an
// arrangement rather than correcting a belief, which is the line
// Callout.astro's docs draw.
//
// Placed BEFORE the scope on purpose. A practice manager's first question is
// not what we clean, it is what we will refuse to touch, and a page that
// buries the answer past three sections looks like it is hoping not to be
// asked.

// §4 The three settings. `ServiceBlocks variant="highlight"` — click a card,
// its panel opens. THE ONLY USE OF THIS VARIANT IN THE CLUSTER, and it is
// here because this page's content is genuinely a selection rather than a
// list: a reader is one of these three and does not need the other two.
//
// ⚠️ EVERY ITEM IS DRAFTED against standard practice, not supplied by TLB.
// Confirm each one and be conservative — medical is the setting where an
// overstated capability does the most damage.
//
// These three items are also the natural split if this URL is ever broken
// into three pages. Kept in one array for that reason.

// §5 The shared scope. `ContentGrid` with `feature` cells — four things that
// are true across all three settings, in a shape that reads as a checklist
// rather than as the six-item grid the office and aged care pages use.
//
// ⚠️ The colour-coding item carries a real bracket. It is the second thing a
// practice manager asks, right after clinical waste.
//
// In `sharedScopeBlocks`, on "Colour-coded equipment" (body):
// ⚠️ Real bracket. Confirm before launch.

// §6 "Worth knowing". On this page the lever is that a clinic is judged on
// cleaning by someone other than its patients — an accreditation surveyor, an
// insurer, a health inspector — and a cheap cleaner leaves no evidence
// behind.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED, and this block is deliberately vague where
// specifics would go stale:
//   - General practices in Australia are accredited against the RACGP
//     Standards, which do require an infection prevention and control
//     approach covering the practice environment. Correct in substance.
//     NO EDITION OR CRITERION NUMBER IS QUOTED — the Standards are revised
//     and a stale citation is worse than none.
//   - Skin penetration premises (tattooing, piercing, some beauty
//     treatments) are separately regulated in NSW and inspected by local
//     councils. Correct, and hedged to "some beauty treatments" on purpose.
//   - Nothing here claims TLB's documentation exists yet. See the file
//     header and branded FAQ 2.

// §7 Related pages. Aged care is appended as an extra — it is the nearest
// neighbour to this page's clinical half, and a reader responsible for a
// clinic inside a village needs both.
const relatedTags = relatedPremises('/commercial-cleaning/medical-and-clinic-cleaning/');

// §9 Branded FAQs, in the CUSTOMER'S voice — a practice manager, clinician
// or salon owner.
//
// In `brandedFaqItems`, on "Will you follow our infection control policy?" (answer):
// ⚠️ Real bracket. An accrediting practice will ask for this document.
// In `brandedFaqItems`, on "Are your cleaners police-checked?" (answer):
// ⚠️ Cluster-wide bracket. See office-cleaning.astro's header.

// §10 Non-branded FAQs, the long-tail block.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED. Checkable claims below:
//   - Clinical waste must be segregated at the point of generation and
//     collected by a licensed contractor. Correct in substance across
//     Australian jurisdictions; no specific regulation is cited on purpose.
//   - Cleaning and disinfecting are two different steps and cleaning must
//     come first because organic soil inactivates most disinfectants.
//     Correct, and the single most useful thing in this block.
//   - Skin penetration premises in NSW are regulated under public health
//     legislation and inspected by local councils. Correct; no section
//     quoted.
//   - No dollar figure anywhere, same as every sibling page.
//
// In `nonBrandedFaqItems`, on "How much does medical or clinic cleaning cost?" (answer):
// ⚠️ BRACKETED ON PURPOSE, same as every sibling page.
// In `nonBrandedFaqItems`, on "Do salons have cleaning regulations in NSW?" (answer):
// Brief §24. Two house rule breaches in one sentence: BFD §9.2 bans em
// dashes outright, and the live version also carried an Oxford comma in
// "tattooing, piercing, and certain", which §9.2 bans as well. The
// recast fixes both, keeps every item, and adds "meaning" so the clause
// still reads as a definition.
//
// This is the only question in either FAQ block that is about salons
// specifically, and it is a good one. If the salon scope question is
// resolved as out of scope, this answer is the one thing worth keeping.

// §11 Closing CTA.

// CONTENT lives in src/content/pages/commercial-cleaning/medical-and-clinic-cleaning.yaml
// and is edited in Pages CMS ("Medical and clinic cleaning"). This file owns the layout. The
// notes above explain why each section says what it says; they stay here
// because Pages CMS rewrites the YAML on save and would drop them.
const page = await getPage(
  'commercial-cleaning/medical-and-clinic-cleaning',
  z.object({
    seo: section.seo,
    // The label of the page's main button. It links to the quote page in Site
    // settings. Empty falls back to the site-wide walkthrough label.
    ctaLabel: section.optional(section.text),
    hero: section.hero,
    // Its background photo sits behind the trust bar and this block together
    // (the Backdrop below), so it cannot be left empty.
    definition: section.textBlock.extend({ backgroundImage: section.backgroundPhoto }),
    waste: section.callout,
    // A setting with no button of its own gets the page's main button.
    settings: section.imageCards,
    sharedScope: section.contentGrid,
    worthKnowing: section.textBlock,
    // The pills are the other premises pages, from Site settings.
    related: section.tagCloud.omit({ tags: true }).extend({ note: section.optional(z.array(section.text)) }),
    reviews: section.sectionHeading,
    // The subheading falls back to the shared footprint line, and the towns
    // come from Site settings.
    whereWeClean: section.tagCloud.omit({ tags: true }).extend({ subheading: section.optional(section.text) }),
    // Heading only. The paragraphs are the shared brand lines.
    brandLine: section.sectionHeading,
    brandedFaq: section.faq,
    faq: section.faq,
    closingCta: section.callToAction,
  }),
);

const pageMeta = {
  ...page.seo,
  canonical: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/commercial-cleaning/medical-and-clinic-cleaning/',
};

const walkthroughCta = { label: page.ctaLabel ?? walkthroughCtaLabel, href: quoteCta.href };

const heroImage = page.hero.image && sized(page.hero.image, '16/9');

const settingItems = page.settings.items.map((item) => ({
  ...item,
  image: item.image && sized(item.image, '4/3'),
  cta: item.cta ?? walkthroughCta,
}));

const sharedScopeBlocks = page.sharedScope.items.map((item) =>
  item.type === 'image' ? { type: 'image' as const, ...sized(item.image, '4/3') } : item,
);

const business = commercialBusiness(pageMeta.description);
---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    <Hero
      variant="split-single-image"
      imagePosition="right"
      kicker={page.hero.kicker}
      headingLines={page.hero.headingLines}
      subheading={page.hero.subheading}
      lead={page.hero.lead}
      cta={page.hero.cta ?? walkthroughCta}
      image={heroImage}
    />

    <Backdrop image={page.definition.backgroundImage} tint="muted" opacity={90}>
      <TrustBar variant="card" cards={trustBarCards} />
      <TextBlock
        theme="muted"
        align="left"
        heading={page.definition.heading}
        body={page.definition.body}
        cta={page.definition.cta}
      />
    </Backdrop>

    {/* §3 — before the scope, not after it. See the file header. */}
    <Callout tone="warning" heading={page.waste.heading} body={page.waste.body} cta={page.waste.cta} />

    {/* §4 — the only `highlight` in the cluster. Three settings the reader
        picks between, because a reader is one of them and does not need the
        other two. Also the natural split if this ever becomes three URLs. */}
    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="highlight"
      heading={page.settings.heading}
      lead={page.settings.lead}
      note={page.settings.note}
      tileCtaLabel={page.settings.tileCtaLabel}
      items={settingItems}
    />

    <ContentGrid heading={page.sharedScope.heading} lead={page.sharedScope.lead} columns={2} blocks={sharedScopeBlocks} />

    <TextBlock
      theme="dark"
      align="left"
      heading={page.worthKnowing.heading}
      body={page.worthKnowing.body}
      cta={page.worthKnowing.cta}
      backgroundImage={page.worthKnowing.backgroundImage}
    />

    <TagCloud
      heading={page.related.heading}
      subheading={page.related.subheading}
      tags={relatedTags}
      note={page.related.note ?? []}
    />

    <GoogleReviewSlider heading={page.reviews.heading} reviews={googleReviews} profileUrl={googleBusinessProfileUrl} />

    <TagCloud
      heading={page.whereWeClean.heading}
      subheading={page.whereWeClean.subheading ?? footprintSubheading}
      groups={footprintLocationGroups}
      note={page.whereWeClean.note}
    />

    {/* §1.3 The Line as a section heading, with §1.4 as its standfirst.
        The copy, and the reason this page gets `fast` rather than the other
        variant, both live in data/brand-lines.ts. Change it there, not here.

        `muted`, not `dark`: TagCloud above paints --color-brand and Faq below
        paints --color-surface, so Cream between them keeps the page
        alternating instead of stacking two dark bands.

        No CTA prop. §9.5 wants one primary CTA per page, repeated, and the
        closing CallToAction is directly under the FAQs. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="muted"
      align="left"
      heading={page.brandLine.heading}
      body={[managedForYou.recurring, ...theLineBody.commercial, ...howItWorks.general]}
    />

    {/* Brief §21 and §22. The page carries two FAQ headings and the generic
        one made the split look like a build error. The second heading also
        lands the mapped phrase contiguously, which is the 10-point H2
        component the page previously half scored, and drops Title Case,
        which BFD §9.2 bans.

        ⚠️ Note, tied to the salon question in the file header: this rename
        drops "salon" from the heading. If salons are confirmed in scope, a
        second FAQ heading covering them is the natural place to put the
        mapped salon terms once they exist. Do NOT add it before the mapping
        does. */}
    <Faq heading={page.brandedFaq.heading} lead={page.brandedFaq.lead} items={page.brandedFaq.items} />
    <Faq heading={page.faq.heading} lead={page.faq.lead} items={page.faq.items} />

    <CallToAction
      variant="secondary"
      kicker={page.closingCta.kicker}
      heading={page.closingCta.heading}
      lead={page.closingCta.lead}
      cta={page.closingCta.cta ?? walkthroughCta}
    />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: footerLogoImg }}
    columns={[
      { title: 'Services', links: footerServiceLinks },
      { title: 'Locations', links: [{ label: 'View all locations', href: '/locations/' }] },
    ]}
    contact={footerContact}
    copyright={footerCopyright}
  />
</Base>
